  "question": "Show binary search for 7 in [1, 3, 5, 7, 9], then give simple Python code.",
  "rawResponse": "[STEP]we want the position of 7 in the sorted values 1, 3, 5, 7, 9. the answer is an index, starting from zero.[/STEP]\n\n[STEP]we could check each value from left to right. that works, but on a long list it might take a check for every value.[/STEP]\n\n[STEP]the first search frame shows all five values still in play.[FOCUS:probe0|spotlight] we start at index 0 and end at index 4, then check the middle because the values are sorted.[/STEP]\n\n[STEP]the next frame shows 5 at the middle, below 7.[FOCUS:probe1|spotlight] 5 loses the comparison, and so does everything to its left. we move the lower boundary to index 3.[/STEP]\n\n[STEP]the final frame shows 7 at index 3.[FOCUS:probe2|spotlight] it matches the target, so we return index 3 after two checks.[/STEP]\n\n[STEP]the search stops there. it never checks 9, and it does not need to: finding 7 has already settled the answer.[/STEP]\n\n[STEP]this section turns those search moves into code. we give the search its values and target, then set low and high, the boundaries around all five candidates.[TYPE:s1b1][/STEP]\n\n[STEP]while candidates remain between those boundaries, we choose their middle index. this gives us the first check at index 2.[TYPE:s1b2][/STEP]\n\n[STEP]if the middle value matches the target, we return its index. that is how the second check stops at 7.[TYPE:s1b3][/STEP]\n\n[STEP]if the middle value is too small, we move low past it. when 5 is below 7, this keeps only indices 3 and 4.[TYPE:s1b4][/STEP]\n\n[STEP]otherwise the middle value is too large, so we move high below it. if the boundaries cross without a match, we return negative one to mean not found.[TYPE:s1b5][/STEP]\n\n[STEP]we run the search on these five values with 7 as the target. it prints 3, the index where 7 sits.[TYPE:s1b6][/STEP]\n\n[STEP]the time is log n because each unsuccessful check discards about half the remaining values. the extra space is constant: for these five values, we only keep a few indices.[/STEP]",
